steps.body:
`nextLine()` returns everything up to the end of the current line, spaces included, and drops the newline. Use it when a value can hold spaces, like a full name or a sentence.

The classic trap: `nextInt()` reads the number but leaves the newline after it in the input. The next `nextLine()` then returns the empty rest of that line, not the line you wanted. The fix is one extra `nextLine()` to throw away the leftover:

```stdin
36
  Ada Lovelace  
```

```java harness
import java.util.*;

public class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) throws Exception {
        Scanner sc = new Scanner(System.in);
        {{code}}
        System.out.println(age);
        System.out.println(name);
    }
}
```

```java
int age = sc.nextInt();
sc.nextLine();                         // consume the rest of the age line
String name = sc.nextLine().strip();   // now reads the real next line
```

Mixing is only a problem in that direction. `next()` and `nextInt()` skip leading whitespace on their own, so they work fine after a `nextLine()`.

Lines often carry stray spaces at the start or end. Call `strip()` on what you read so `  Ada  ` becomes `Ada` before you print or compare it.

