}

steps.body:
When the input starts with a count, read `n` first, then loop `n` times. Use an `int[]` when you know the size, or an `ArrayList` when you want to grow it:

```stdin
3
4 9 2
```

```java harness
import java.util.*;

public class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) throws Exception {
        Scanner sc = new Scanner(System.in);
        {{code}}
        System.out.println(Arrays.toString(a));
    }
}
```

```java
int n = sc.nextInt();
int[] a = new int[n];
for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {
    a[i] = sc.nextInt();
}
```

When there is no count, read until the input ends. `hasNextInt()` looks at the next token without consuming it and returns `false` at the end of input, or when the next token is not an int:

```stdin
4 9 2
7
```

```java harness
import java.util.*;

public class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) throws Exception {
        Scanner sc = new Scanner(System.in);
        {{code}}
        System.out.println(nums);
    }
}
```

```java
List<Integer> nums = new ArrayList<>();
while (sc.hasNextInt()) {
    nums.add(sc.nextInt());
}
```

The same pattern works for other shapes. `while (sc.hasNext())` with `next()` reads words, and `hasNextLine()` with `nextLine()` reads whole lines:

```stdin
  first line  

second line
```

```java harness
